   "error": null,
   "response_text": "Found it, fixed it, verified it. \ud83d\udc36\n\n**Cause:** In `src/click/types.py`, `BoolParamType.convert` stripped surrounding whitespace into `norm` but did *not* lowercase it before checking membership in the accepted-word sets:\n\n```python\nnorm = value.strip()\n\nif norm in {\"1\", \"true\", \"t\", \"yes\", \"y\", \"on\"}:\n    return True\n\nif norm in {\"0\", \"false\", \"f\", \"no\", \"n\", \"off\"}:\n    return False\n```\n\nSo `TRUE`, `Yes`, `Off`, etc. never matched and fell through to the `\"is not a valid boolean\"` failure.\n\n**Fix:** one-token change \u2014 lowercase the normalized value:\n\n```python\nnorm = value.strip().lower()\n```\n\nThis handles both requirements at once: `strip()` ignores surrounding whitespace, `lower()` makes the word lookup case-insensitive. Numeric forms (`1`, `0`) and the `{False, True}` fast path are untouched.\n\nVerified conversions: `TRUE`, ` True `, `Yes`, `Off`, `OFF`, `FaLsE`, `  oN  ` all resolve correctly, and the change is confined to the single library source file (no test files touched).\n\nDONE",
